#!/usr/bin/env python3

# Usage: admin/ct-vartime [target...]
#
# The constant-time check for instructions whose timing depends on their
# operands, which ctgrind, `admin/ct-sde`, and `admin/ct-qemu` cannot see.
# It builds rind's release rlib for each Linux target in TARGETS, every
# architecture rind builds for, or those of them named, and disassembles it
# with every crate it links, the standard library and compiler-builtins included. Starting from
# every function named for rind, it follows each relocation, so calls, tail
# calls, and the function pointers in vtables, through to code the compiler
# generated. It fails if a function it reaches holds an instruction in
# VARTIME or calls a routine in HELPERS, unless `admin/ct-vartime.toml`
# lists exactly what that function holds on that target, with why its
# operands are public, or if an entry there does not match what is found.
#
# All of rind counts as a secret path, so no entry point needs marking, and
# a new instruction in a function the list names still fails. It stops at the panic routines
# in PANICS, which run only once a check has failed. It cannot see code that
# only rind's callers compile, such as a generic function at a type rind
# never instantiates itself, nor the C library's memcpy and memset.
#
# On the x86_64 backend, it also fails if a function reached from one rind
# exports, which is all its callers can call, holds an instruction in MCDT
# that can run outside Intel's MXCSR mitigation, `rind::low::Mcdt`. In a
# function that calls MITIGATION, it follows the control flow, so an
# instruction, and whatever a call there reaches, counts as mitigated only
# where every path from the function's start calls MITIGATION with no call
# in RELEASE since: the mitigation must dominate it. Nothing in `admin/ct-vartime.toml`
# excuses these, public operands included: the mitigation costs little
# beside the arithmetic that runs them. A generic function compiles in its
# caller, which exports what it calls, so the mitigation goes in code that
# compiles in rind, such as a trait's method for one type. The portable
# backend's arithmetic may compile to these anywhere, so on x86 every
# `rind::low::Entry` enters the mitigation, and the scan skips it: what it
# exports for generic callers, such as trait impls, has no `Entry` of its
# own.
#
# The build sets `--cfg rind_vartime_planted`, which adds the divisions of a
# secret in `rind/src/low/vartime_planted.rs`, and for the x86_64 backend a
# multiply inside the mitigation and three outside it: in a function that
# never enters it, and before and after the mitigation in one that does.
# Each outside must be found, so a scan that finds nothing fails, and the
# one inside must not.
#
# The `+mul16` targets build Poly1305's products from 16-bit halves. build.rs
# sets that for the Thumb-M triples, for ARMv4T and ARMv5TE (`rind_mul16_top`,
# bit 31 set on each half), and for RISC-V without the M extension
# (`rind_mul16_soft`, a shift-and-add). See `rind/src/low/generic/mul16.rs`.
# The Thumb triples are scanned as themselves, so Thumb-1 and Thumb-2 cannot
# hide a long multiply that armv7's A-profile code would not emit.
# `armv5te-unknown-linux-gnueabi` is the top-bit path; `armv4t`, `thumbv4t`,
# `thumbv5te`, and `arm-linux-androideabi` set the same cfg and the first
# three have no prebuilt standard library here. `riscv32i-unknown-none-elf`
# is the shift-and-add path; `riscv32e` sets the same cfg and has no prebuilt
# standard library. armv7's portable build with the cfg forced is scanned
# too, without the top or soft cfg, so its halves stay plain `mul`s. From
# each function in MUL16, the scan follows calls as above and fails on a
# 32x32->64-bit multiply, a call to a multiply routine, or, on RISC-V, a
# multiply instruction. It must find the one planted. On ARMv5TE it also
# requires every `mul` and `mla` in `blocks` and `finish` to take both
# operands from `orr #0x80000000`: a plain `mul` of a raw half, or a `mul`
# by five, ends early on ARM7TDMI and XScale, and the planted copies of
# those must be rejected. On RV32I it rejects a conditional branch in those
# functions whose condition is a masked bit, the shape a select on the
# secret half lowers to, and the planted branch must be rejected. The
# `+branch` row runs only that branch proof, on `riscv32imac`, whose 26-bit
# limbs multiply with `mul` and `mulhu` but whose `finish` still selects
# between h and h - p with a mask. A `none`
# target gets getrandom's custom backend, because a library build cannot
# name one.
#
# `admin/ct-vartime --self-test` checks those two proofs on fixtures and
# does not build.
#
# Run it in `nix develop .#ct-vartime`, which has llvm-objdump and each
# target's standard library.

